''Dacrydium cupressinum'' is the only member of the genus ''[[Dacrydium]]'' native to New Zealand.{{sfn|Vennell|2019|pp=132–137}} A 1998 study on the [[phylogeny]] of the Podocarpaceae family examined the [[evolution]] of ''D.&nbsp;cupressinum'' and various other species. The research identified it as sister to a [[clade]] (group) consisting of various other Podocarpaceae species, including members of the genus ''[[Dacrycarpus]]'' and ''[[Podocarpus]]''. This discovery revealed that ''D.&nbsp;cupressinum'' shares a common biological ancestor with these genera, while maintaining its evolutionary uniqueness.{{Sfn|Kelch|1998|pp=2, 7}}

A 2020 study suggested that the ''Dacrydium'' genus originated in [[New Caledonia]], based on a historical biogeographic reconstruction. Despite this, macrofossil evidence strongly supports a more broader [[Australasia]]n origin, and another study also suggests that ''Dacrydium'' originated in Australasia during the [[Late Cretaceous]] epoch and dispersed into [[Southeast Asia]] in the [[Early Oligocene]].{{sfn|Khan|Hill|Liu|Biffin|2023|p=41}}{{sfn|Morley|2011|p=11}} Stull et al. 2021 investigated the role of plant evolution, analysing how ecological changes have shaped biodiversity and trait development over long timescales. In the study, they revealed that [[polyploidy]] is a primary driver of microevolution in plants, but its broader impact on macroevolutionary diversification and phenotypic innovation remains uncertain. In the study, researchers produced an ancestral reconstruction of [[gymnosperm]]s using [[RNA-Seq|RNA sequencing]].{{sfn|Stull|Qu|Parins-Fukuchi|Yang|2021|pp=1, 15}} ''D.&nbsp;cupressinum'' is the [[type species]] of the [[genus]] ''Dacrydium''.{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}}{{Sfn|Farjon|2017|p=348}}

===History===
The name ''D.&nbsp;cupressinum'' was first published [[Nomen nudum|invalidly]] in 1786 by the European naturalist [[Georg Forster]], using a description by [[Daniel Solander]], in a publication entitled, ''De Plantis Esculentis Insularum Oceani Australis Commentatio Botanica''.{{Sfn|Forster|Solander|1786|p=80}}{{Sfn|IPNI|2025}} The British botanist [[Aylmer Bourke Lambert|Aylmer Lambert]] provided the first valid [[species description|description]] of the species in his 1806 work, ''A Description of the Genus Pinus''.{{Sfn|Lambert|Don|Bauer|1806|p=93}} The name, ''Thalamia cupressina'', comes from a later 1826 description of ''D.&nbsp;cupressinum'' where [[Kurt Polycarp Joachim Sprengel|Kurt Sprengel]] attempted to place the taxon, ''D.&nbsp;cupressinum'' into the genus, ''Thalamia''. His placement has not been accepted by other plant taxonomists, and hence the name given by Sprengel becomes a homotypic synonym.{{Sfn|POWO|2025}}{{Sfn|Linné et al.|1826|p=890}} In 1855, French botanist [[Élie-Abel Carrière]] described the species ''Dacrydium cupressiforme'', in his work {{Lang|fr|Traité Général des Conifères}}.{{sfn|Carrière|1855|p=494}} What he thought to be a new species is now recognised as being ''Dacrydium cupressinum'' and hence is a heterotypic synonym of the species described earlier in 1806.{{Sfn|POWO|2025}} 

===Etymology===
The [[etymology]] (word origin) of ''D.&nbsp;cupressinum''{{'}}s genus name, ''Dacrydium'', comes from the [[Greek language|Greek]] {{lang|el|dakryon}} (δάκρυδον), which translates to 'tear-drop' or simply 'tear';{{sfn|Gledhill|2008|p=142}}{{sfn|Farjon|2017|p=348}} one possibility, suggested by Dutch botanist [[Aljos Farjon]], stated that the genus name could be in reference to the [[resin]]ous drops exuded by the tree.{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}}{{sfn|Farjon|2017|p=348}} However, English-born botanist [[Thomas Kirk (botanist)|Thomas Kirk]], in his 1889 revision titled ''The Forest Flora of New Zealand'', instead hypothesised that ''Dacrydium'' is in reference to the weeping habit of the trees.{{Sfn|Kirk|1889|pp=31, 330}} The [[specific epithet]] (second part of the scientific name), {{lang|el|cupressinum}}, translates simply to 'cypress'; which refers to the tree's similarity with ''[[Cupressus]]'' species.{{sfn|Vennell|2019|pp=132–137}} ''D.&nbsp;cupressinum'' is [[common name|commonly known]] as rimu.{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}}{{sfn|Farjon|2017|p=356}} The word rimu (or limu) is typically used in [[Polynesian languages]] to describe seaweeds and mosses; it is likely that the name was applied to the tree due to the similarity of the tree's leaves to seaweed.{{sfn|Vennell|2019|pp=132–137}}

